\par\par \b \uc2 Latvians would like peg to stay, but see devaluation likely-poll\b0\par\par\uc2 259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2009\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 RIGA, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Almost two thirds of Latvians want the lat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the euro to remain unchanged, but more than 60 percent believe devaluation is likely, the Baltic news agency BNS said on Monday citing data released by pollster SKDS.\par \par \uc2 The lat has come under pressure amidst rumours of an imminent devaluation but has firmed since mid-July, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy or sell euros last week, for a third week in a row, as confidence in the lat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained.\par \par \uc2 The poll, which was conducted in mid-July, showed 64 percent of respondents saying they would like the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the current peg to the euro, while 14 percent said they would like to see a devaluation, the news agency reported.\par \par \uc2 Still, 10 percent of respondents believe devaluation was imminent, while 24 percent said the probability was high and 28 percent said it was a possibility, the survey showed.\par \par \uc2 SKDS confirmed poll figures to Reuters in a phone call.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly ruled out devaluation, but it was not immediately available to comment on the poll.\par \par \uc2 The lat, which is pegged to the euro with a 1 percent range, was quoted at 1355 GMT on Monday at 0.7015/7025 to the euro <EURLVL=>, close to the central rate of 0.7028.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jorgen Johansson)\par \par \uc2 LATVIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \