\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Latvian mkts stay nervy, eyes on budget and IMF\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Lannin   \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2009\par 09:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds European Commission statement, treasury \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 RIGA/LONDON, June 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lat hit an almost 3-month high against the euro on Thursday, boosted by reports of government buying and speculation that support from international bodies might help fend off a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Bank-to-bank interest rates in the small Baltic state rose to a record 25 percent, however, and nerves are still strained over the prospect of a cut in the lat's value that would hammer Swedish banks and reignite concerns over the fate of eastern Europe in the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 A failed government debt sale on Wednesday added to the surge in interbank lending costs and comments from Swedish and other officials this week, intensifying speculation that devaluing is the only way to cure its collapsing economy.\par \par \uc2 But Latvia's leaders have stood firm behind the peg for months, even as the economy shrank by a fifth year-on-year, and on Thursday there was no sign of them backing down.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Bank of Latvia\uc2  has chosen a certain policy, defining it and explaining to society and is carrying it out," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Bank of Latvia\uc2  has explained the negative effect of a devaluation on people, companies and the solvency of the state."\par \par \uc2 The lat firmed away from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels with dealers citing purchases of lats by the Treasury.\par \par \uc2 By 1020 GMT, the euro <EURLVL=> was quoted at 0.7070/80 lats, well away from the 0.7098 level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the lat within a 1 percent fluctuation band to the euro.\par \par \uc2 "The reason for the rise is that first of all the market is quite tight and the state treasury has also been doing some euro-lat trades," said one trader.\par \par \uc2 The treasury had no immediate comment.\par \par \uc2 BUDGET KEY\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Valdis Dombrovskis said on Wednesday he must reach a deal swiftly on the release of more IMF and EU funds under last year's 7.5 billion euro ($10.72 billion) bailout, in the hope that will calm nerves around the country's finances.\par \par \uc2 A payout has been linked to further cuts in spending to account for a worsening economic picture and the 2009 budget faces a first reading in parliament on Thursday, with other cuts still being discussed.\par \par \uc2 "European Economic and Monetary Affairs Commissioner Joaquin Almunia believes that the new budgetary proposals are a step in the right direction and also appear to contain some welcome reforms," the European Commission said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "But more is needed to contain the increase in the budget deficit."\par \par \uc2 The Commission said the International Monetary Fund (IMF), whcih led the pair's bailout of Latvia last year, would also make a statements on work on the 2009 budget.\par \par \uc2 Liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been strained by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying lats to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its one percent band against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Overnight deposit rates were bid as high as 25 percent <LVLOND=>, Reuters charts indicated, up from the previous day's peak of 24 percent.\par \par \uc2 Given the high overnight rates, the euro fell to 0.7084/94 lats, away from the level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it, though it later edged back again to 0.7088/98, with 0.7098 euros being the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level.\par \par \uc2 One money market trader said lat liquidity might ease in the coming days as banks had borrowed more money from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday than the previous day.\par \par \uc2 Data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  <LVBANK11> showed that on Wednesday banks borrowed a total 213.2 million lats ($426.5 million) from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mostly via the marginal lending facility. This was up from 119.8 million lats on Tuesday. (Reporting by Patrick Lannin, Jamie McGeever and Marcin Grajewski, writing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 LATVIA-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G|DA|FN|NW|SW|BNX|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090604e564000h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
