\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Latvia \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk: \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Over For Time Being\b0\par\par\uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2008\par 13:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest round of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is over "for the time being," \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monetary Policy Head Karlis Bauze said Friday.\par \par \uc2 "During the previous two weeks we did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Bauze said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was over "for the time being," after an investment of around EUR170 million designed to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's euro peg.\par \par \uc2 The Latvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fixed at a central parity of 0.702804 and allowed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate plus or minus 1%.\par \par \uc2 Bauze said the Latvian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to revise down its current growth forecasts on the financial market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 "We do those forecasts twice a year," he said. "There will be revisions made particularly in line with the current market conditions."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Latvia\uc2  currently predicts gross domestic product growth of 0.5% on the year in 2008, 1.0% on the year in 2009 and 3.1% on the year in 2010, well below the double digit growth recorded in 2007.\par \par \uc2 "We are looking vigilantly at what's happening in the financial markets," the central banker said. "We are in turbulent times."\par \par \uc2 But he said current investor appetite for risk wasn't affecting Latvia's ability to borrow.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment the government doesn't need to borrow as it can get investment from the European Investment Bank," Bauze said. Because of this, "risk adverseness is not directly affecting the government's ability to borrow," he said.\par \par \uc2 And he said he thought Latvia's current account deficit will unwind gradually, narrowing to below 10% of gross domestic product in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Latvia's current account deficit was 15.6% of the country's GDP in the second quarter, sparking concerns that the Baltic country would be vulnerable to tight credit markets resulting from the recent financial turmoil, as it borrowed to finance growth.\par \par \uc2 But Bauze said he expected the "external imbalances to unwind gradually."\par \par \uc2 Speaking at an investment event, he said the current account deficit would "diminish gradually" and "fall below 10% in 2009".\par \par \uc2 -By Emma Charlton, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-20-7842-9307; emma.charlton@dowjones.com [ 17-10-08 1136GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081017e4ah000kb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
