\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-C.bank chief sees soft landing in Latvia  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Lannin and Jorgen Johansson  \par \uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 RIGA, March 20 (Reuters) - The head of Latvia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that he expected a soft landing of the economy and warned that anyone speculating against the lat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would get their fingers burnt.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview, Ilmars Rimsevics said some of the recent comments about his small Baltic nation showed a lack of understanding about its economy, and some writers appeared not even to know where it was.  \par \par \uc2 Latvia has attracted much attention in recent weeks, sparked by a devaluation rumour that swept the country in mid-February. The lat has since then been stuck at the lowest end of its range, near 0.7098 to the euro <EURLVL=>.  \par \par \uc2 But Rimsevics told Reuters: "It is difficult, it is challenging, it is not a crisis."  \par \par \uc2 Ratings agencies have warned about Latvia's high inflation, more than 7 percent so far this year, and have pointed to a large current account deficit, of more than 20 percent of gross domestic product (GDP).  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently raised a key interest rate, the refinancing rate, to 5.5 percent from 5.0 percent and last week spent 65 million euros to buy lats on the market.  \par \par \uc2 "If you interview any economist or central banker I think there will be very few who would as a first option mention a hard landing. I definitely believe that we will have a soft landing," Rimsevics said.  \par \par \uc2 He said some people wanted to speculate against the lat, which is fixed to the euro and is in the ERM-2 grid.  \par \par \uc2 Such speculation had happened during a bank crisis in 1995 and in the emerging markets meltdown three years later, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Of course they burnt their fingers in '95, they burnt their fingers in '98 and they will burn their fingers in 2007," said Rimsevics, who has been at the bank 15 years and has been its head since 2001.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be devalued, which is what they are betting on," he said. Some speculators still had positions in the lat, he added.  \par \par \uc2 He also hit out at some analysts and commentators, who he said had shown a lack of knowledge about the region.  \par \par \uc2 "When we have seen some articles where Latvia has been described as a country from the Balkans, then that gives me the right and also the chance to say that some people don't even know where Latvia is," he said.  \par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT INFLATION PLAN  \par \par \uc2 Rimsevics shared some of the concerns expressed by the ratings agencies, but said the economy was "getting first aid".  \par \par \uc2 He cited the government's recently launched plan to bring down inflation, which includes the fiscal tightening of moving the budget to balance this year and next and then to surplus.  \par \par \uc2 New real estate deal taxes will also be introduced to cool the property market, where prices have soared amid strong lending growth by the banks.  \par \par \uc2 He said the government's action plan was lacking in some respects, but overall he thought it was credible.  \par \par \uc2 "It is maybe a little bit delayed, but if carried out properly, without any hesitation, first of all on the fiscal front, it will reach the numbers envisaged," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Rimsevics indicated there was not much the bank itself could do on monetary policy, even if it raised rates last week.  \par \par \uc2 He said this served more as a signal of concern about inflation rather than a practical instrument as banks did not really use the refinancing rate.  \par \par \uc2 The government expects inflation to fall to 2.0 to 2.5 percent by 2011. Rimsevics said this indicated "that 2012 is the most realistic date when \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2  could introduce the euro".  \par \par \uc2 He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the lat should be seen in the context of purchases of 1 billion euros last year to stop the lat appreciating to the top of its band.  \par \par \uc2 LATVIA-CBANK/ (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070323e33k000xv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
