\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Latvia  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  supported lat last week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Burton Frierson \par \uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2000\par 15:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  RIGA, Nov 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week for the first time in a year to support the lat and keep it within its trading limits against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  "We started last week in small amounts. We started on November 28," Andrejs Viks, a portfolio manager at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the lat, which is pegged at 0.7997 to the IMF's SDR world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, was weakened by the repatriation of funds from a bank buyout and year-end cash flows. \par \par \uc2  Viks said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $3.5 million as the lat touched the lowest levels permitted in its plus or minus one percent band. It was the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since December 3, 1999. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally when market rates overshoot its own bid and offer spreads, which represent the strongest and weakest points of the band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only discusses its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the Monday following any activity on the market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced into a spate of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in summer 1999 amid mounting budget deficit worries. \par \par \uc2  The lat has been stronger this year thanks to a tighter fiscal stance by the government. \par \par \uc2  Martins Jaunarajs, short-term liquidity manager at Hansabanka, attributed the lat's weakness to year-end cash flows. "I don't think people are going short because they fear a devaluation or interest rate hikes." \par \par \uc2  He said the market was usually volatile in December following a pre-holiday buying spree by retailers of imported goods and as banks hurried to square positions by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  This year the market has also been drained of lats by a $54 million buyout of shares in leading bank Unibanka by Sweden's SEB. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they thought SEB used money market borrowing to handle most of the purchase, which would produce no net inflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while foreign and some other shareholders have been tempted to chase investments abroad amid a stagnant period for Latvian equities. \par \par \uc2  "There was about 20 million lats ($32 million) that was exchanged into dollars or euro in the last few weeks," said one dealer. "That money will most probably will go to other markets." \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 0.622 Latvian lats). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwc40bd0b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
