\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Latvia seals IMF deal, seen boosting lat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Burton Frierson \par \uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1999\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  RIGA, Sept 15 (Reuters) - The IMF and Latvia announced on Wednesday an agreement on an economic policy programme, with promises of deficit reduction that analysts said would boost the beleaguered lat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but may stymie growth. \par \par \uc2  Latvia's Finance Minister Edmunds said IMF approval of the country's policies should convince markets the government had the strength to defend a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has been sagging amid budget deficit worries. \par \par \uc2  The deal allows Latvia to tap World Bank structural adjustment loans (SAL) at favourable rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed the deal should boost investor confidence, but they said the proof would come when the government launched a planned 75 million euro ($77.5 million) bond issue in October. \par \par \uc2  "The main objective of this programme is to lay the foundation for sustained economic growth," IMF representative Adalbert Knobl told a news conference, adding that the IMF sees GDP growth near one percent this year and around four percent next year. \par \par \uc2  "This is to be achieved through two main instruments. The first is to provide for macroeconomic stability through fiscal and monetary restraint that is also supporting the fixed exchange rate of the lat," he said. Structural reform was the second focus. \par \par \uc2  Knobl said the government had agreed to reduce the fiscal deficit from slightly under four percent of GDP this year to under two percent next year, to press ahead with privatisation and boost minority shareholder rights. \par \par \uc2  Improving the regulatory environment, particularly in the energy sector, and banking supervision were also key structural issues to be worked on under the agreement, Knobl added. \par \par \uc2  BORROWING FLEXIBILITY \par \par \uc2  Krastinsh said the IMF programme and stamp of approval gave the government more flexibility in its borrowing plans, and deficit reduction would boost the lat. \par \par \uc2  "What can really put minds at ease is the cutting of budget deficit for 2000 to two percent," Krastinsh said. \par \par \uc2  "The agreement with IMF...will allow us to use the World Bank's structural adjustment loan, pushing any possible borrowing (beyond the October issue) of international markets further down the line. This would happen - if at all necessary - no sooner than before next summer," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the deficit reduction would strengthen the defence of the lat, which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Latvia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to support through sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's important that they have an agreement on the fiscal deficit with an international institution like the IMF," said Erste Bank analyst Petra Frewein. \par \par \uc2  The lat was weak on Wednesday, with dealers saying there were signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it had been off the market for a week. They said the IMF news would not effect trade drastically in one day. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, Frewein said the fiscal tightening would not speed recovery for an economy that contracted by 2.3 percent in the first quarter, and the key test of investor confidence would be the October euro bond issue. \par \par \uc2  "I think it will show us if there is a change in the mind of investors... Recently the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have decreased. Perhaps we are moving in a direction where investors are trying to believe in the lat again," Frewein said. \par \par \uc2  ($1=.9671 Euro) ((Riga Newsroom, +371 7222 2079, fax: +371 722 2079, reuters@reuters.lv)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9f00iup\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
